# verify/compose-3 — independent verification of perf/st1-kernels + perf/lr-neon-tier

host: Apple M4 Pro (8P+4E), macOS 26.5.2, 24 GB. Release (`lto=fat`), DEFAULT
features, NO `-C target-cpu=native`, NO `nice` on any timed run. Builds niced.
base: `verify/compose-2` @ 2e0ea4d.
composed: `verify/compose-3` — 2e0ea4d + perf/st1-kernels (a5c50e5) +
perf/lr-neon-tier (edb55a1) + f7bda07 (one fix, below) + dff0ef2 (harness).
reference: dav1d 1.5.4 (homebrew), measured in the SAME interleaved sweep as
both rav1d arms, at BOTH `--framedelay 1` and its shipping default.
raw: `verify_compose3_gap_2026-08-08.tsv` (4K grid, 220 rows),
`verify_compose3_lrgap_2026-08-08.tsv` (LR-active, 216 rows),
`verify_compose3_scaling_2026-08-08.tsv` (thread-scaling map, 336 rows).
Summariser: `scripts/perf/gap_summarize.py` — every table below re-derives
from those TSVs by running it.


## 1. BOTH BRANCHES MERGE AND VERIFY. Nothing was left out.

`perf/st1-kernels` and `perf/lr-neon-tier` touch disjoint source (ipred vs
looprestoration+tracker+decode) and merged with zero conflicts. Both are on
`origin` at the SHAs their reports claim.

One defect found by inspection and fixed in the compose (f7bda07):
**`perf/st1-kernels` silently deleted `cfl_ac_rust`'s `#[inline(never)]`.** It
inserted the test-only `cfl_ac_scalar_for_test` alias between the attribute and
its function; attributes bind to the NEXT item, and that alias is
`#[cfg(all(test, ...))]`, so in every non-test build the attribute was removed
along with the item it had been re-parented onto. Correctness-neutral,
profile-visible — and the same branch's own trap #5 is about using
`#[inline(never)]` to make a scalar fallback attributable.


## 2. GAP TO dav1d — the standing 4K grid

ms/frame, median of 5 interleaved rounds, arm order rotated per round, every
committed round had foreign>25%CPU count = 0. Two-point fit (2 and 20 frames).

  vector          t     base     comp   dav1d_fd1  dav1d_def  comp/base  vs fd1
  v4k_8tile       1   401.28   399.06     246.67     246.50      0.994    1.618
  v4k_8tile       2   217.39   215.39     125.11     125.78      0.991    1.722
  v4k_8tile       4   116.61   115.22      65.56      63.89      0.988    1.758
  v4k_8tile       8    72.11    71.06      34.39      32.89      0.985    2.066
  v4k_8tile_10b   1   439.06   435.72     251.39     251.78      0.992    1.733
  v4k_8tile_10b   2   245.22   246.06     127.61     128.67      1.003    1.928
  v4k_8tile_10b   4   129.61   129.50      66.78      65.39      0.999    1.939
  v4k_8tile_10b   8    83.94    83.83      36.11      34.78      0.999    2.322

The `base` column reproduces `verify_compose2_2026-08-08.meta`'s `composed`
column to within 1.4% at every cell, so the two campaigns are comparable.

**~1.3x is met at NO cell.** Best 1.62x, worst 2.32x. The compose buys
0.6-1.5% on the multi-tile grid, and only the two t=1 cells have disjoint
per-round ranges (8bpc base [400.89..406.22] vs comp [394.67..400.78]; 10bpc
base [437.56..439.89] vs comp [431.78..437.00]). **Every t=2/4/8 cell here has
overlapping ranges and is NOT resolvable at n=5** — including the 10bpc t=8
cell that `perf/st1-kernels` printed as 88.0 -> 85.6 (2.29x -> 2.22x) without
marking it unresolvable; its own raw TSV has base [85.50..91.11] and head
[84.89..91.50], and its own trap #3 says between-run drift is 5x the win.


## 3. THE SINGLE-TILE REGRESSION IS FIXED — at a smaller magnitude than claimed

  vector          t     base     comp   dav1d_fd1  comp/base  ranges
  v4k_1tile       4   369.28   353.44     240.28     0.957    disjoint
  v4k_1tile       8   369.22   352.56     240.06     0.955    disjoint
  v4k_1tile_10b   8   405.56   377.28     242.06     0.930    disjoint

Confirmed, all three disjoint. But `perf/lr-neon-tier` reported v4k_1tile t=8
as 383.72 -> 362.28 (0.944) and "1.59x -> 1.51x". My base measures **369.22,
not 383.72** — 3.8% faster — so the fix is worth **4.5%, not 5.6%**, and the
resulting ratio is **1.54x -> 1.47x, not 1.59x -> 1.51x**. Their `pre` arm was
their own branch minus the gate commit; v4k_1tile does zero LR, so it should
have equalled compose-2 and did not. Direction and rough size reproduce; the
exact delta does not. (Better for them on the ratio, worse on the delta.)


## 4. THE `--framedelay 1` REFERENCE FLATTERS US 3x ON SINGLE-TILE CONTENT

`verify_compose2_2026-08-08.meta` states: "dav1d's own default is within 4% of
its `--framedelay 1` figure at every cell (0.97-1.01x), so the like-for-like
restriction is not what the remaining 2x is made of."

**That is true on the multi-tile grid and false off it, and the compose-2 grid
had no single-tile cell to notice.** Measured in the same sweep:

  vector          t   dav1d_fd1  dav1d_def  def/fd1     comp vs def
  v4k_8tile       8       34.39      32.89    0.956          2.160
  v4k_1tile       4      240.28     115.33    0.480          3.065
  v4k_1tile       8      240.06      77.89    0.324          4.526
  v4k_1tile_10b   8      242.06      77.78    0.321          4.851

dav1d's shipping answer to a single-tile frame is FRAME threading, which
`--framedelay 1` switches off and the checked rav1d-safe build cannot do at all
(n_fc is forced to 1). Against dav1d as it actually ships, single-tile 4K at
t=8 is **4.53x (8bpc) / 4.85x (10bpc)**, not 1.47x/1.56x. Most AVIF stills are
single-tile. The campaign's headline ratio is a tile-threading-only ratio and
should say so.


## 5. LOOP RESTORATION: first gap-to-dav1d numbers ever taken on it

The standing grid does ZERO loop restoration, so no gap number has ever seen
it. `scripts/perf/verify_gap_ivf.sh` + `examples/bench_ivf_limit` put both
rav1d arms and dav1d on the same IVF with the same whole-process clock.
Median of 9 rounds, foreign=0.

  vector                      t    base    comp  dav1d_fd1  comp/base   vs fd1
  8-bit/data/00001147         1  215.50  198.00      39.00      0.919    5.08x
  8-bit/data/00001147         8  187.50  183.50      21.50      0.979    8.53x
  10-bit/issues/318_tx_4x4    1    9.17    8.70       2.00      0.949    4.35x
  10-bit/issues/318_tx_4x4    8   25.73   25.70       0.63      0.999   40.58x
  8-bit/data/00000645         1    5.76    5.65       0.77      0.981    7.37x
  8-bit/data/00000855         1   33.67   33.33       2.67      0.990   12.50x

The NEON tier is real and reproduces: 00001147 t=1 base [214.00..218.50] vs
comp [196.50..201.00], disjoint, -8.1%; 318_tx_4x4 t=1 -5.1%, disjoint. Those
are comp-vs-compose-2, a different comparison from the branch's own
neon-vs-ablated-scalar A/B (0.876 / 0.941), and land in the same place.

**And the gap to dav1d on this content is 4.3x to 12.5x, two to six times worse
than anything the 4K grid shows.** The campaign has been optimising against its
easiest vector.


## 6. TWO THREAD-SCALING INVERSIONS, BOTH PRE-EXISTING, BOTH UNREPORTED

Median of 7, foreign=0. Same instrument as §5.

  10-bit/issues/318_tx_4x4        base    comp   dav1d_fd1   comp step   vs fd1
    t=1                           9.30    8.83        2.03       --       4.34x
    t=2                           9.23    9.20        1.13     1.042      8.12x
    t=4                          10.30    9.33        0.67     1.014     14.00x
    t=8                          25.97   25.57        0.63     2.741     40.37x

  8-bit/data/00000645
    t=1                           5.79    5.68        0.78       --       7.33x
    t=2                           6.70    6.61        0.69     1.164       9.55x
    t=4                           6.67    6.56        0.70     0.992       9.37x
    t=8                           6.73    6.59        0.69     1.005       9.53x

* **318_tx_4x4 t=4 -> t=8 is a 2.74x SLOWDOWN.** comp [9.00..9.93] at t=4 vs
  [25.17..26.57] at t=8 — not close to overlapping. Present identically in the
  base, so the compose did not cause it. dav1d goes 0.67 -> 0.63 over the same
  step.
* **00000645 t=1 -> t=2 is a 16% slowdown** ([5.62..5.79] vs [6.40..6.71],
  disjoint) that never recovers at 4 or 8.
* The multi-tile 4K grid has NO inversion at either depth (every t=N -> t=2N
  step < 1.0), which is exactly why one grid was not enough to find these.
* 00001147 scales 1.08x from t=1 to t=8 — no inversion, no scaling either.

The tile gate landed in §3 does not touch these: it makes the block shift
CONSTANT for single-tile frames, and at t=8 comp/base is 0.985-0.999 on both.
Whatever the t=8 cliff is, it is upstream of the shift choice.


## 7. CORRECTNESS — 766/766, set-diffed BY NAME with the actual MD5 in the key

`examples/md5_inventory` over all 768 dav1d-test-data vectors, joined on
`group/name \t status \t actual_md5`, sorted, `comm`'d against
`benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07_final.tsv.zst`:

  768 rows both sides | 0 only-in-ref | 0 only-in-new | 766 PASS / 2 SKIP

Run FOUR times on this tree: plain; `--features __simd_test` (which re-runs the
scalar loop-restoration reference after every call and PANICS on divergence —
a stricter gate than the branch's `__simd_test_log`); `--features
__simd_test,__lrvarcov`; and `--features __simd_test,__lrpoison`. All four
identical. `__lrvarcov` printed all TEN (bit depth, variant) pairs, so no LR
variant went unexercised.

Both gates PROVEN LIVE BY MUTATION, not assumed:

* **M4** — `+8` pre-shift (== +1 post-shift) in `wiener_hor_8bpc`'s VECTOR lane
  only, scalar tail untouched: 48,673 `LR_MISMATCH` lines and 325 of 358
  vectors flipped to MISMATCH on `8-bit/data` alone. Restored, green.
* **M5** — the 8bpc `boxsum` loop shortened by one destination row, so the
  neighbour pass reads a scratch cell the call never wrote: the `__lrpoison`
  and non-poison builds produced DIFFERENT corpus digests
  (`f7c5dc51a6888ef7d67b5858cc34a0b8` vs `0bb942d27e54c166ef3f53572730a4fe`,
  both differing from the clean `7e88460c08f62540193a97b8ac2ff58d`). That is
  what makes the clean poison run evidence: the fill genuinely reaches the
  never-cleared per-thread scratch, so the reuse argument is checked.


## 8. SOUNDNESS

`#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` verified ACTIVE by planting an `unsafe { }` block in
BOTH new SIMD modules (**M3**): the build failed with
`error: usage of an unsafe block` pointing at
`looprestoration_arm.rs:83` and `ipred_arm.rs:6`. Restored; zero `unsafe` and
zero `allow(unsafe_code)` added by either branch.

Tracker detection re-proved by mutation on the COMPOSED tree:

* **M1** — the 4af62ae TOCTOU: delete the in-lock `state` re-read from `add`'s
  single-block fast path. `wide_exclusion::a_wide_borrow_excludes_every_narrow_shard`
  FAILED in 0.02 s with **115** violations.
* **M2** — `BorrowTracker::active()` shortened to `&self.shards[..1]`. Same test
  FAILED with **13** violations.
* Both restored; `git diff --exit-code` clean against HEAD after each; full
  tracker suite green (36 lib + soundness 27 + wide_exclusion + narrow_release
  + shard_liveness + pic_buf_overflow + aligned_miri).

Guard extents unchanged. `perf/lr-neon-tier`'s tracker edit is confined to
shift SELECTION (`block_shift_rule` + a new monotone `OBSERVED_TILES` latch);
no borrow-path code changed. `set_tile_concurrency` is called in
`rav1d_submit_frame` at decode.rs:5131, before `rav1d_thread_picture_alloc` at
5235 — the deadline is real and met, since the shift is read once in
`BorrowTracker::new`.

Stress, all on the composed tree:
* `mt_stress_4k` at threads 1/2/4/8/16 x 5 trials — ok.
* `tile_threading_parity` 3/3, `thread_cleanup_test` 2/2, `cancellation` 6/6.
* **Concurrent multi-decoder pressure** (`scripts/perf/multi_decoder_pressure.sh`,
  new): 16 then 3 x 24 concurrent decoder PROCESSES, thread counts cycling
  1/2/4/8/16 over five vectors, hard wedge deadline. 4/4 rounds PASS, every md5
  equal to the serial reference, no panic, no wedge.


## 9. PRE-EXISTING TEST FAILURE, NOT CAUSED BY THIS COMPOSE

`cargo test --release --test decode_permutations -- --test-threads=1`:
**3 passed / 16 FAILED, byte-identical pass/fail sets on the base and on the
composed tree** (`diff` of the two `^test test_` listings is empty).

Cause: 23 `Arm64::summon().unwrap()` sites in `src/safe_simd/mc_arm.rs` (e.g.
line 5931) and `src/safe_simd/filmgrain_arm.rs` (458, 695). The permutation
harness disables archmage tokens process-globally to exercise the scalar
fallbacks; those two modules unwrap instead of falling back, so they panic.
**Not a production risk** — NEON is architecturally guaranteed on aarch64, so
`summon()` cannot return `None` on real hardware. It IS a coverage hole: the
test that claims to exercise "every dispatch gate in every SIMD module at every
tier" cannot cover mc or filmgrain on this arch, and has not been able to for
as long as those unwraps have existed. Everything else in
`cargo test --release --workspace` passes.


## 10. WHAT IS STILL OWED, RANKED BY ABSOLUTE ms AT THE WORST CELL

Owed = composed minus dav1d in the same sweep.

  v4k_1tile_10b  t=8  377.28 - 242.06(fd1) = 135.2   / - 77.78(def) = 299.5
  v4k_1tile      t=8  352.56 - 240.06      = 112.5   / - 77.89      = 274.7
  v4k_8tile_10b  t=1  435.72 - 251.39      = 184.3
  v4k_8tile      t=1  399.06 - 246.67      = 152.4
  00001147       t=1  198.00 -  39.00      = 159.0   (5.08x)
  v4k_8tile_10b  t=8   83.83 -  36.11      =  47.7   (2.32x)
  v4k_8tile      t=8   71.06 -  34.39      =  36.7   (2.07x)
  318_tx_4x4     t=8   25.57 -   0.63      =  24.9   (40.4x)

Where that 152.4 ms at v4k_8tile t=1 lives, per `st1_kernels_2026-08-08.meta`'s
`sample` buckets (218.5 ms entropy / 96.8 ms tracker / 85.7 ms pixel kernels)
— which I did not re-take, but whose dav1d entropy figure I DID trace to
`p2_kernel_profile_2026-08-07.meta` (215.4 ms symbol-resolved, at a dav1d wall
of 246.3 ms/frame, within 0.05% of the 246.67 I measure today, so the citation
is sound and not stale):

  **Correction to that branch's conclusion.** It writes "Zeroing EVERY pixel
  kernel leaves 315 ms = 1.28x. Kernel work alone cannot reach the target at
  t=1." 1.28x is INSIDE 1.3x, so the arithmetic contradicts the sentence. The
  defensible version is the one that uses dav1d's own kernel cost rather than
  zero: entropy 218.5 + tracker 96.8 + dav1d-parity kernels 31 = **346.3 ms =
  1.41x**. Kernel parity is not sufficient; drop the tracker too and it is
  249.5 ms = **1.01x**. The tracker is the binding constraint — that conclusion
  survives; the argument for it did not.


## 11. VERDICT

**materially_closer.** The compose is a real improvement and it is sound and
bit-exact, but ~1.3x is met at no thread count on any vector measured, and the
two new instruments this verification added (LR-active content, single-tile
content against shipping dav1d) BOTH show the gap is materially worse than the
standing grid says: 4.3-12.5x on loop-restoration content, 4.5-4.9x on
single-tile 4K against `dav1d` as it ships, and one 2.74x thread-scaling
inversion at t=4 -> t=8 that predates both branches.


## 12. TRAPS FOR THE NEXT SESSION

1. **A whole-process comparison arm must not hash.** `bench_ivf_limit` started
   with the md5 inside the timed region against `dav1d --muxer null`, which
   hashes nothing. On 318_tx_4x4 that was 8.5 of 17.0 ms/frame — the gap read
   8.5x when it is 4.3x. Found only because the number disagreed with the LR
   A/B's 9.1 ms/frame on the same vector. Cross-check every new instrument
   against an existing measurement of the same thing before believing it.
2. **One vector family is not a grid.** `v4k_8tile{,_10b}` does no loop
   restoration, has 8 tiles, and is one repeated key frame. Every one of §5,
   §6 and §4 is invisible to it, and two of the three are severe.
3. **`dav1d --framedelay 1` is the like-for-like arm, not the reference the
   user is comparing against.** Always run `dav1d_def` beside it; on
   multi-tile content they agree within 4% and on single-tile they differ by
   3.1x.
4. **Overlapping per-round ranges are not a result.** At n=5 on this box most
   sub-2% deltas on the 4K grid are unresolvable. `gap_summarize.py` prints
   min/max per arm per cell for exactly this reason; read it before writing a
   ratio into prose.
5. **`decode_permutations` is red on `main`'s lineage** and has been. Do not
   spend an hour bisecting it into your own change — diff the pass/fail SET
   against the base first (it takes 55 s per side).
6. **Attributes bind to the next item, and `#[cfg(test)]` takes the attribute
   with it.** Inserting a test-only helper directly under an `#[inline(never)]`
   silently strips it from the release build. Neither `cargo build` nor clippy
   says a word.
